DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       ssyymmlliinnkk()  creates  a  symbolic  link named _l_i_n_k_p_a_t_h which contains the
       string _t_a_r_g_e_t.

       Symbolic links are interpreted at run time as if the  contents  of  the
       link  had  been substituted into the path being followed to find a file
       or directory.

       Symbolic links may contain _._.  path components, which (if used  at  the
       start of the link) refer to the parent directories of that in which the
       link resides.

       A  symbolic  link  (also known as a soft link) may point to an existing
       file or to a nonexistent one; the latter case is known  as  a  dangling
       link.

       The permissions of a symbolic link are irrelevant; the ownership is ig‐
       nored  when following the link (except when the _p_r_o_t_e_c_t_e_d___s_y_m_l_i_n_k_s fea‐
       ture is enabled, as explained in pprroocc(5)), but is checked when  removal
       or  renaming  of  the  link is requested and the link is in a directory
       with the sticky bit (SS__IISSVVTTXX) set.

       If _l_i_n_k_p_a_t_h exists, it will _n_o_t be overwritten.

   ssyymmlliinnkkaatt(())
       The ssyymmlliinnkkaatt() system call operates in exactly the same  way  as  ssyymm‐‐
       lliinnkk(), except for the differences described here.

       If  the  pathname given in _l_i_n_k_p_a_t_h is relative, then it is interpreted
       relative to the directory referred to by the file  descriptor  _n_e_w_d_i_r_f_d
       (rather  than  relative to the current working directory of the calling
       process, as is done by ssyymmlliinnkk() for a relative pathname).

       If _l_i_n_k_p_a_t_h is relative and _n_e_w_d_i_r_f_d is  the  special  value  AATT__FFDDCCWWDD,
       then  _l_i_n_k_p_a_t_h is interpreted relative to the current working directory
       of the calling process (like ssyymmlliinnkk()).

       If _l_i_n_k_p_a_t_h is absolute, then _n_e_w_d_i_r_f_d is ignored.

       See ooppeennaatt(2) for an explanation of the need for ssyymmlliinnkkaatt().

